## Mutant A — `time` reply prefix `utc: ` -> `utc= ` (kernel/src/shell.rs)

Mutated line: `con.puts("utc: ");` -> `con.puts("utc= ");`
Rebuild: `cargo build --release` (Finished release profile in 4.41s) +
`bash scripts/build-disk.sh release`. Run:

    MUTANT_A_RC=1
      FAIL  `time` printed `utc: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS` (window: b'\r\r\nutc= 2026-09-29T04:09:08\r\r\nnova> ')
    shell driver: FAIL (1 failed check(s): `time` printed `utc: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS`)
    FAIL: interactive shell oracle (see transcript above; serial log: /c/Users/surya/Documents/dont-share-personal-info/build/shell-drive-serial.log)

KILLED. The parser's whole-match requirement failed against the guest's
actual reply — caught from the guest's own bytes, not from kernel source.

## Mutant B — dispatch arm `"time"` renamed to `"timer"` (kernel/src/shell.rs)

Mutated line: `"time" => {` -> `"timer" => {` (only the match arm name; the
RTC body is unchanged, so the verb still works when invoked). Rebuild
(Finished in 4.12s) + disk rebuild. The host oracle greps dispatch arms from
shell.rs, so it typed `timer`; help still advertises `time`:

    MUTANT_B_RC=1
      FAIL  help lists `timer`
      FAIL  `time` was in the typed verb set (K3 scope: CMOS RTC)
    shell driver: FAIL (2 failed check(s): help lists `timer`; `time` was in the typed verb set (K3 scope: CMOS RTC))
    FAIL: interactive shell oracle (see transcript above; serial log: /c/Users/surya/Documents/dont-share-personal-info/build/shell-drive-serial.log)

KILLED. Note the mechanics: the guest accepted `timer` (its reply is the RTC
body), so a naive "recognized" check would have passed — the rename is
caught by the source-derived verb list crossing the guest's help output.

## Revert and post-revert sanity

    git checkout -- kernel/src/shell.rs    # tree clean (git status empty)
    cargo build --release                  # Finished release profile in 4.05s
    bash scripts/build-disk.sh release     # pristine image rebuilt

Post-revert full run: 11 checks ok, `guest utc 2026-09-29T04:15:58` epoch
1790655358, host bracket 1790655109..1790655134 (+/-120), PASS — see
`docs/logs/k4a-shell-drive-check-full.log` stage 12 for the in-check rerun.

## What this does NOT prove

- reboot/halt/novatest are excluded from typing (they end the session);
  their arms are only exercised as source-derived exclusions, not at runtime.
- The bracket check tolerates +/-120 s; a guest clock wrong by less than the
  tolerance in the wrong direction could pass stage 12 while failing the
  tighter stage-10 rtc gate. Stage 10 remains the tight clock oracle.
